  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "What is the body clock?",
      "a": "The body clock is a group of about 20,000 nerve cells in the brain, called the suprachiasmatic nucleus, that keeps a roughly 24-hour rhythm. It times sleep, hormones, temperature and hunger.",
      "asks": [
        "What is a circadian rhythm?",
        "Where is the body clock?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How does light reset the body clock?",
      "a": "Special light-sensitive cells in the retina, separate from the ones used for seeing, detect daylight and signal the master clock. Morning light nudges the clock to match the real day.",
      "asks": [
        "Why does daylight wake you up?",
        "How do eyes set the body clock?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is melatonin?",
      "a": "Melatonin is a hormone released by the pineal gland in the brain when it gets dark, under the control of the body clock. It signals to the body that it is night-time and helps you feel sleepy.",
      "asks": [
        "What makes us sleepy at night?",
        "What does the pineal gland do?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why do I get jet lag?",
      "a": "After a fast flight across time zones, your body clock is still on home time, so you feel sleepy, hungry or awake at the wrong times. Daylight in the new place gradually resets it, about one time zone a day.",
      "asks": [
        "What causes jet lag?",
        "How long does jet lag last?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why is jet lag worse flying east?",
      "a": "Flying east means you must shift your clock earlier, but most people's natural rhythm runs slightly longer than 24 hours, so it is easier to stay up later than to go to sleep earlier.",
      "asks": [
        "Is jet lag worse going east or west?",
        "Why is flying east harder?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "The master body clock, the suprachiasmatic nucleus, contains about 20,000 nerve cells.",
    "The word circadian comes from Latin for \"about a day\".",
    "Melatonin is made by the pineal gland, mostly in darkness."
